# Bastion's leaders: the commanding officer at the stronghold and the outpost commanders.
# Voice: clipped, procedural, tired; close with its own. Nobody is coming; they're all they have.
# Lines are spoken straight to the player (docs/WRITING.md, "Conversations"): who they are, where
# you are and what's going on, in their own words.

# The first time you meet one (a greet: the talk screen opens on it). One line, said only to a
# stranger (!met); the season's name (names.rzd) and a caught lie come first when they apply.
conv bastion.leader.hello
for stronghold:bastion, seat:bastion/outpost_lead
when !met
greet
once
prio flavour
voice bastion

node start
  say? stronghold & !met :: I'm {npc}, {role}. Command stopped answering on Day 2. Since then this stronghold, its posts and the families on our roster are all Bastion has. Keep your weapon slung in here.
  say? kit:other & !met :: I'm {npc}, {role}. You're at a Bastion post in someone else's colours, so a sentry has had a rifle on you since the gate. Keep your hands where they are and we'll talk.
  say? !met :: I'm {npc}, {role}. This is a Bastion post: soldiers left inside the line when it closed, and the families who stayed with us. Weapon slung inside the wire. Then we can talk.

conv bastion.leader.roster
for stronghold:bastion, seat:bastion/outpost_lead
topic "What's that board on the wall?"
prio world
voice bastion

node start
  say That's the roster. Every name at this post and every duty, in grease pencil. Shifts change at oh-five-thirty and seventeen-thirty.
  say A name on that board eats, stands a watch if it can, and gets looked for if it doesn't come back. The dead stay on it. We read them all at the roll.
  say? stronghold :: Lieutenant Sedlak keeps it. On Day 6 he wrote the families from his Day 2 column onto it, under civilians attached. They've been ours since.
  > @rubout Do you ever rub a name off it? -> rubout
  > @roll What happens at the roll? -> roll
  > @soldier Regs say the dead come off the board. -> soldier   if bg:soldier,police
  > @on Could my name go on that board? -> sign
  > @other Let me ask you something else. -> hub

node rubout
  say One kind of name. A man listed KIA who walks back in through the wire. Then we rub out the KIA, not the man.
  say It's happened. Not often. Every time it did, the whole post came out to look at him, like he was weather.
  > @back1 Good. Let me ask something else. -> hub

node roll
  say When the night watch is set, the sergeant reads the whole board out loud. The living answer here. For each of the dead, the whole post answers together.
  say The roll gets longer every week. Nobody's suggested we shorten it. Then the operator calls Command, and Command says nothing.
  > @why1 Why read the dead at all? -> soldier
  > @back0 I see. Something else, then. -> hub

node soldier
  say Procedure says a lot of things. It was written by people who meant to come back for us. On Day 5 we watched the north road barrier get relieved in eleven minutes. Not for us.
  say Nobody is coming. So the names stay up, and we answer for them. We're all we have.
  do learn:bastion.roster
  > @back2 Understood. Let me ask something else. -> hub

node sign
  say? tier:bastion>=trusted :: You're on it already, under civilians attached. Somebody wrote you in last week. Nobody asked you, and nobody needed to.
  say Civilians get signed on. Families, mechanics, a nurse at one post. You'd stand a watch, eat what we eat and answer the roll. That's the whole contract.
  > @why What would it get me? -> what
  > @think I'll think about it. -> hub

node what
  say A name on a board, and a squad that comes looking when you don't answer at twenty-thirty. We've walked into overrun towns for names on that board.
  say Out here that's most of what anyone has. The Haven gives you soup. We give you somebody who won't stop looking.
  > @back3 That's worth something. Something else. -> hub

conv bastion.leader.command
for stronghold:bastion, seat:bastion/outpost_lead
topic "Does Command ever answer you?"
prio flavour
voice bastion

node start
  say Every night, after the roll, the operator reads our numbers to Command. Effective strength, rounds, fuel, civilians sheltered. Last of all: requesting relief.
  say Then the operator writes no reply in the log, and the sergeant chalks up another day on the wall. Every night since Day 2.
  > @know When did you know they weren't coming? -> know
  > @stop Why not stop calling? -> stop
  > @other4 Let me ask about something else. -> hub

node know
  say Day 5, oh-five-thirty. Corporal Vrana was on the hill over the north road barrier with binoculars. Four trucks with our badge on the doors came up the far side.
  say Fresh men took the sandbags, the old ones climbed aboard, and they drove away. A relief in place, by the manual. Lieutenant Sedlak logged it at oh-five-forty-one: not for us.
  > @go Go on. -> know2
  > @back5 That's enough. Something else. -> hub

node know2
  say? stronghold :: That night I read it to Command myself, with the numbers. No reply. Next shift change I spoke to every post. Nobody is coming. We feed our own, look for our own, bring our own home.
  say That night the major read it to Command, with the numbers. No reply. Next shift change the major spoke to every post. Nobody is coming. We feed our own, look for our own, bring our own home.
  say Nobody walks out on the rest. That's been the order ever since, and it's the only one that's never been late.
  > @stop2 And still you call every night? -> stop
  > @back6 I understand. Something else. -> hub

node stop
  say Every post hears every other post call in. As long as the calls go out, nobody's the last one left. A post that reads rations, one day, usually has a truck from here by morning.
  say The first post that stops calling is the first post that's alone. Not us. Not tonight.
  > @back4 Fair enough. Let me ask something else. -> hub
